 ["Abdominal exam — palpable pelvic mass if fibroids are large, may feel irregular/nodular ('bosselated' uterus)","Bimanual pelvic exam — enlarged, irregular, mobile uterus typically","Assess for pallor if associated heavy bleeding causing anaemia","Assess size in relation to gestational-equivalent weeks (a common way fibroid uteri are described clinically)"],
 ["Rapid increase in size, especially in a postmenopausal woman → raises concern for leiomyosarcoma (rare), needs urgent imaging/gynae oncology input","Severe acute pelvic pain with a known fibroid, especially in pregnancy → consider red degeneration","Significant anaemia from heavy bleeding → correct urgently alongside definitive management planning","Any postmenopausal bleeding alongside fibroids → still needs endometrial assessment, don't attribute to fibroids alone"],
 ["Pelvic ultrasound (transabdominal/transvaginal) — confirms number, size, and location of fibroids","CBC to assess for anaemia from heavy bleeding","MRI pelvis if planning surgical/interventional treatment (better anatomical mapping) or diagnosis unclear","Endometrial biopsy if abnormal bleeding pattern raises concern for coexisting endometrial pathology"],
 "Per NICE NG88 (Heavy menstrual bleeding) and ACOG guidance on fibroids: management depends on fibroid size/location, symptom severity, and fertility plans; options range from watchful waiting (asymptomatic/small fibroids) to medical management (LNG-IUS if cavity not significantly distorted, tranexamic acid, GnRH agonists for pre-operative shrinkage) to surgical options (myomectomy for those wanting to preserve fertility, uterine artery embolisation, or hysterectomy for those who have completed family and have significant symptoms).",
 ["Asymptomatic small fibroids: reassurance and routine follow-up, no treatment needed","Symptomatic with heavy bleeding: LNG-IUS (if cavity not significantly distorted), tranexamic acid, or combined hormonal contraception as medical options","GnRH agonists can be used short-term pre-operatively to shrink fibroids and correct anaemia before surgery","Myomectomy for women wishing to preserve fertility with symptomatic fibroids","Uterine artery embolisation or hysterectomy considered for those who have completed childbearing with significant symptoms not responding to medical management"],
 ["Tranexamic Acid (for heavy bleeding) Transamin 1g TDS during heavy bleeding days. Non-hormonal option, useful adjunct while planning definitive treatment","Levonorgestrel IUS (if cavity not significantly distorted) Mirena Inserted by trained provider. Effective for bleeding control, may not be suitable if large submucosal fibroids distort the cavity","GnRH agonist (pre-operative, specialist-initiated) Zoladex (Goserelin) 3.6mg SC monthly for 3 months pre-op. Shrinks fibroids and corrects anaemia before surgery; used short-term due to menopausal side effects","Ferrous Sulfate (if anaemic) Ferrous Sulphate/Fefol 200mg OD-BD. Correct anaemia alongside definitive management"],
